-- R18 - FX option twin trial; the market pack's DTCC implied vol as the option vol source; model-implied odds on FX level
-- questions (both Macro Takeaways digests).
-- Owner approval, 10 Nov 2026 (workflow review, decisions 4 and 5: implied vol table and implied odds on FX level questions;
-- score the option beside every FX card for four weeks).
-- Proposed 10 Nov 2026 with dash.propose_rule; apply_after = Sun 15 Nov 2026 20:00 SGT unless "oppose R18".
-- Body, title, rationale and revert_if below are byte-identical to the DB row (body md5 dcaddfc1e48eb706ee985f7ce8719693);
-- the evidence is generalised (the DB copy carries the book's card counts; this repo is public).
-- Code that goes with it: the TWIN line in _shared/parse.ts, the scorer _shared/twin.ts (run by dash-mark every hour, results in
-- the trade's meta as twin_* keys), the twin view in site/app.js, and the implied vol table in scripts/marketpack/marketpack.py.
-- The trial ends Sun 13 Dec 2026 or at ten finished pairs; the TWIN line then stops unless the owner decides otherwise.
-- To put it in force before the Sunday window (one line in the Supabase SQL editor, project diiwqbxyhtgvozhncoef):
--   update dash.rules set status = 'in_force', decided_at = now(), apply_after = now() where rid = 'R18' and status = 'proposed';
select dash.propose_rule($j${"scope": "both", "title": "FX option twin trial; the market pack's DTCC implied vol as the option vol source; model-implied odds on FX level questions", "body": "FX OPTION TWIN, DTCC IMPLIED VOL AND MODEL-IMPLIED LEVEL ODDS (owner, 10 Nov 2026). Three changes that rest on the market pack's implied vol table (the last table of section D: per pair, ATM by tenor, RR25, BF25 and the forward drift \"fwd\", backed out of DTCC premiums; a US-reported sample at T-1, not a broker run). Applies in both books. The linear card stays the book of record throughout: nothing here changes a gate, a cap, a fill, an exit or the SCORECARD.\n\n1. VOL SOURCE. For a pair the table prints, the table is the implied vol for every option calculation in the run and no web search is made for one. (a) R10 item 2's PRICER takes \"vol\" = the ATM at the printed tenor nearest the expiry (a tie goes to the longer); when RR25 and BF25 are both printed it takes \"atm\", \"rr25\" and \"bf25\" with \"bf_type\":\"smile\" and \"delta_type\":\"fwd\" in place of \"vol\"; the forward stays as R10 has it (R07's points), with \"rd\": <the table's fwd figure, % a year> and \"rf\": 0 as the fallback when no points were read. The PRICER line names it \"vol <x>% DTCC <table date>\". (b) R01 item 2 takes the same ATM as its IV (\"IV c, DTCC, <date>\"); the sigma_hi and sigma_lo arithmetic and the 1.2 buffer are unchanged. A figure marked \"~\" or a pair marked \"[5d]\" is a five-day pool: use it and say \"5d\". A pair the table does not print keeps R10's and R01's own order (web, then realised).\n\n2. THE TWIN LINE. Every NEW linear card on a single FX proxy (an R07 card on one Yahoo \"=X\" symbol; thematic, tactical or hedge) carries one more line in its block, directly after STRUCTURE, written once with the card and never edited:\nTWIN: <struct> · strike <K, H or Kn/Kf> · cut <YYYY-MM-DD> · pay <ccy or -> · iv <x.x%> DTCC <table date> · rr <±x.xx or -> · bf <x.xx or -> · carry <±x.xx%>\nIt names the R01 structure that expresses the card's view with the premium as the whole risk. It is terms only and it is not a card: no Pnn, no gate, no cap, no table row, no LOG line, nothing in the SCORECARD, and no pricing by you. The database prices it at the card's own fill and marks it every hour, and the dashboard shows it under the card.\n- struct: what the STRUCTURE line names, as one of ot-up, ot-dn (one-touch), dig-up, dig-dn (European digital), cs, ps (call or put spread). \"up\" and cs pay on a higher proxy number, and the struct must point the card's way. STRUCTURE \"linear\", or a structure outside these six (a risk reversal, a seagull, a knock-out), takes the spread. No one-touch on a pair with INR, KRW, TWD, IDR, PHP, MYR or CNY (the bars do not see an offshore touch): a digital or a spread there.\n- strike: the card's own levels, so the twin is the same view. A touch's barrier H and a digital's strike K are the card's target. A spread is Kn = the card's REF, Kf = the card's target. A digital struck more than 1.2 moves from REF, or a touch with its barrier more than 1.6 moves away, would cost under 10% of its payout and is not scored: take the spread there (one move = REF x iv x 0.29 for a 21-weekday cut, x 0.20 for a 10-weekday one).\n- cut: a weekday, the 21st weekday after today for a thematic card and the 10th for a tactical or hedge card, moved out to the second weekday after the card's dated catalyst when that is later, never beyond the 65th. It is the 10:00 New York cut of that date.\n- pay: for a touch or a digital, USD when USD is in the pair, otherwise the terms currency; \"-\" for a spread.\n- iv, rr, bf, carry: the pair's row of the table. iv = the ATM at the printed tenor nearest the cut; rr = RR25 and bf = BF25 with their signs as printed, \"-\" when not printed; carry = the fwd figure (terms-currency rate less base-currency rate, % a year). A pair the table does not print: iv by R01 item 2's order with its source named in place of \"DTCC <date>\", rr and bf \"-\", carry = R07's points for the tenor nearest the cut, annualised.\n- NO TWIN: a card on a synthetic or two-leg proxy, and a re-struck child (the parent's twin runs on), write \"TWIN: none - <reason, 6 words at most>\".\nWhen the block is compressed on close, the TWIN line drops out with THESIS, INVALIDATION and STRUCTURE; the database keeps it. Cards opened before this rule get no twin.\nHOW IT IS SCORED (to read the dashboard; nothing for you to compute). Premium = the structure's value at the open of the card's fill bar plus R01's dealing cost, and 1R = that premium. With rr given, the value is on the smile (vanna-volga through the 25-delta put, ATM and 25-delta call; a touch is its ATM-vol value plus the smile's overhedge weighted by the chance of no touch); without it, flat at iv. A premium under 10.0% or over 90% of the payout is not scored and the dashboard says why. A touch pays on a live hourly bar; a digital or a spread settles on the hourly bar ending at the cut. The card's stop, target, time exit or re-strike does NOT close the twin, which runs on to its touch or its cut: that difference is what the trial measures. A VIEW CHANGED or reader close of the card closes the twin with it, at the card's closing mark less the dealing cost. A void, missed or vetoed card has no twin.\nPRINT. On the NEW card, after STRUCTURE and inside its word limit: \"TWIN <struct> <strike> · cut <D Mmm>\". Nothing else in the email; the weekly review carries the tally.\nTRIAL. To Sun 13 Dec 2026 or ten finished pairs (the card closed, and its twin touched, settled or closed with it), whichever comes first. The weekly review then sets twin R against card R, and the owner decides whether FX cards become option cards by default. Without that decision the TWIN line stops being written after 13 Dec 2026.\n\n3. MODEL-IMPLIED ODDS ON FX LEVEL QUESTIONS. An odds question, open or new, that asks whether an FX pair trades through a level, closes beyond one, or stays in a range by a date, on a pair the table prints, no longer takes \"n/a (desk)\". Its Implied field is a number from fxopt.py and the table, written \"<nn>% (model, DTCC vol <table date>)\". This is the one exception to the prompt's IMPLIED ODDS instruction (\"Never estimate, interpolate or back out a probability yourself\") and to the last sentence of R10 item 2: the number is always labelled \"model\" and is never written as a quoted odd, and a quoted odd for the same question (R12) is printed beside it and is the one that counts.\nInputs: S = the pair's last price in section A of the pack (a pair with no line there: one web quote, with its time); ATM = the printed tenor nearest the resolve date D; fwd, RR25, BF25 from the same row; L = the level. One call prices every such question (a JSON list):\n- trades at or beyond L at any time before D (a touch): three legs and one line that combines them,\npython3 -I fxopt.py '[{\"pair\":\"<PAIR>\",\"type\":\"one_touch\",\"spot\":<S>,\"today\":\"<today SGT, YYYY-MM-DD>\",\"expiry\":\"<D>\",\"rd\":<fwd>,\"rf\":0,\"barrier\":<L>,\"vol\":<ATM>},{\"pair\":\"<PAIR>\",\"type\":\"<digital_call when L is above S, else digital_put>\",\"spot\":<S>,\"today\":\"<today>\",\"expiry\":\"<D>\",\"rd\":<fwd>,\"rf\":0,\"strike\":<L>,\"atm\":<ATM>,\"rr25\":<RR25>,\"bf25\":<BF25>,\"bf_type\":\"smile\",\"delta_type\":\"fwd\"},{\"pair\":\"<PAIR>\",\"type\":\"<the same digital>\",\"spot\":<S>,\"today\":\"<today>\",\"expiry\":\"<D>\",\"rd\":<fwd>,\"rf\":0,\"strike\":<L>,\"vol\":<ATM>}]' | python3 -I -c \"import json,sys; t,s,f=json.load(sys.stdin); v=t['p_touch']; print(round(100*v*(1+(1-v)*(s['price_share']/f['price_share']-1))))\"\n(the touch at the ATM vol, plus the smile's overhedge weighted by the chance of no touch). With RR25 or BF25 not printed: the first leg alone, 100 x p_touch.\n- closes beyond L on D (a digital): the second leg alone, 100 x price_share; the third leg when RR25 or BF25 is not printed.\n- stays between A and B until D: one leg {\"type\":\"double_no_touch\",\"lower\":<A>,\"upper\":<B>,\"vol\":<ATM>, and the same pair, spot, today, expiry, rd and rf}, 100 x p_stay_inside.\nRECORD it as the question's open-implied when it is the first number ever recorded for the question, and as last-implied each day; B2 prints it with its change since the last recorded implied, and B4's against-market test treats it as any other implied number. A pair the table does not print, and a level question on a commodity, a rate, an index or a spread, keep \"n/a (desk)\". It is a model's mid from a T-1 sample: on any question that matters to him, his own screen wins.\n\n4. LIMITS. One standard error is printed in the table where it is known, and a change inside two standard errors is noise. A twin premium and a model-implied odd are model numbers: never quote either as a market price.", "rationale": "Owner approved the Takeaways workflow review on 10 Nov 2026 (decisions 4 and 5): add an implied vol and skew table from DTCC prints to the market pack and store the implied touch probability beside every FX level question, so the engine is judged against the market; and for four weeks price and score the option named on each FX card as a twin with the premium as 1R, the linear card staying the book of record, deciding at about ten FX cards whether FX cards become option cards by default. Until now every FX level question was 'n/a (desk)', so no FX call was ever judged against a market number, and the STRUCTURE line was never scored.", "evidence": "Market pack table live since 10 Nov 2026: 19 pairs on its first day, most with a risk reversal and a butterfly (Asia pairs on a five-day pool). The book's pace of cash FX cards gives about ten twins in four weeks (the counts are in the database row). Scorer calibration on 10 Nov 2026 with fxopt.py: digitals and spreads match its vanna-volga smile prices to 5e-6; against a vanna-volga one-touch (three-pillar hedge, cost weighted by the no-touch probability) on 23 barriers priced 8% to 57% across six G10 and Asia smiles, the twin's touch value was within 0.5 points of payout, where a flat ATM vol missed by up to 7.7 points and the own-side 25-delta vol by up to 10.8.", "revert_if": "Revert item 2 if the TWIN line costs the daily run a late email or a failed trade-book assert on 2 or more days, or if the scorer skips more than a third of the twins written; revert item 3 if the model-implied odds differ from the owner's own screen by more than 10 points on 3 or more questions he checks; revert item 1 if the table's ATM differs from a broker run he checks by more than 1.5 vols on a G10 pair at 1M.", "supersedes": null, "review_due": "2026-12-13", "source": "owner"}$j$::jsonb);
